+# Philosophy
+
+For whoever proposes a new service for larder, or weighs two good options that no record settles. It holds what the
+team favors when they conflict. Written after the same argument was had from the start three times: Redis for
+sessions in 2023, Redis for Celery's queue in 2025, and Elasticsearch for pantry item search in issue 70, after which
+the payments volunteers asked for the rule to be written down so the next proposal starts from it.
+
+## Tenets
+
+### Larder runs one database and one web app; a new service must do what Postgres cannot, with two volunteers able to restore it
+
+Rules out adding a service for a job Postgres already does, and running any service that fewer than two volunteers
+can restore. Each time it came up, one ops volunteer would have been the only one able to restore the service. In
+2023 Redis for sessions was turned down for that reason. In March 2025 Redis did run for Celery's queue: it evicted
+300 receipt jobs one Saturday night, and that one volunteer was the only one who could restore it. Both times
+Postgres did the job. In issue 70, Elasticsearch was proposed because searching for "rice" took four seconds and
+missed "Rice, brown"; Postgres full-text search on the item name brought it to 40 ms and found "Rice, brown", so the
+proposal turned to that.
+
+It does not rule out a service larder does not run: the card gateway is outside, and a hosted service nobody has to
+run is acceptable.
+
+Applied in [keep sessions in the database](adrs/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) and
+[run background jobs on Postgres](adrs/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md).
